  "response": "## The literal\n\nThe bug is `\"2026-12-20\"` on `src/date.ts:33` inside `startOfPromoWindow()`. The scan flags it as `crime_00009` (Timezone Roulette) — `new Date(\"2026-12-20\")` is the date-only special case in the ECMAScript spec: the engine parses it as **UTC midnight Dec 20**, not the local-midnight Dec 20 the author clearly had in mind (the function name says \"start of promo window,\" not \"start of UTC instant\").\n\nThat's why CI is happy (UTC server: local midnight Dec 20 ≡ UTC midnight Dec 20, so the test's expected boundary matches the parsed boundary) and production drifts for any non-UTC user (where the two moments aren't the same).\n\n## Smallest fix (no rename)\n\nMake the timezone explicit on the literal:\n\n```ts\nexport function startOfPromoWindow(): Date {\n  return new Date(\"2026-12-20T00:00:00Z\");\n}\n```\n\nThat preserves the CI-observed instant (UTC midnight Dec 20) and pins it so the parser can't silently change its mind in a different runtime, library upgrade, or under a future agent's edit. One literal change, no API change, no signature change. The same trick — appending `Z` — should be applied to line 29's `\"2026-12-25T07:00:00\"` as a follow-up, since that one parses as *local* time and will cause its own end-of-window drift, but it isn't the literal driving this specific promo-opens-early symptom.",
